KW  - Adolescents
KW  - School
KW  - Academic
KW  - Absenteeism
AB  - Background
Volunteering has positive implications for numerous youth outcomes, including development, health, and employment. However, studies do not examine associations with academic indicators using recent, nationally representative data from the United States.
Methods
Using National Survey of Children’s Health data (2021–2022), we examined the relationship between volunteering and four academic outcomes in a nationally representative sample of youth ages 6–17 (n = 59,404). Logistic and ordered logistic regression models assessed relationships between volunteering and chronic absenteeism, repeated grades, caring about school, and homework completion.
Results
Children whose parents reported volunteering showed significantly lower parent-reported odds of chronic absenteeism (OR = 0.81, 95% CI 0.68–0.95) and repeated grades (OR = 0.58, 95% CI 0.49–0.69), and higher parent-reported odds of caring about school (OR = 1.52, 95% CI 1.41–1.64) and completing homework (OR = 1.58, 95% CI 1.47–1.70). No interaction effects with age, race, or ethnicity were found.
Conclusion
Strong co-occurring patterns between parent-reported volunteering and academic engagement suggest opportunities for school-community partnerships, while highlighting the need for longitudinal research to understand developmental pathways and family characteristics that foster both civic engagement and academic success.
